Qualification: this is what some players want.  You would be in a far
better position than I to know what players want (since you see
everything that players ask for, and I only see what's posted here),
but lacking that information, I have to read 'most' as 'some'.

> Only first-timers and wimps actually pay the bribe, and they're
> not classes of players you claim to be tuning the game for.

Someone else has already responded, but the viral method of "carry ten
gold pieces, bribe the demon lords, move on" is spreading rapidly.  I
pay the bribe because it's the safest and simplest thing to do; a demon 
who never attacks me in melee doesn't summon other demons, doesn't cast
nasty stunning spells... doesn't do anything hostile, in fact.  He just 
goes away.  And it's easy to make sure ten gold pieces are out of the 
bag until all the DLs are bribed.

Once everyone has learned the ten-gold-piece trick, then where the
current complaint is "demon lords aren't hard enough", it now becomes
"demon lords can be ignored".

> So making bribery harder is actually just a way to make quick
> ascension harder, and you've claimed more than once that you're
> not trying to tune conducts.

Well, making bribery harder isn't about making bribery harder; it's
about actually presenting the player with a meaningful choice.  The
original intent appears to have been "part with something you want or
fight a hard monster" -- I'd like to restore those encounters to that in
some fashion.  I am not under any delusion that it will be easy.  :)

(The subset of players you mention above, who want Asmodeus in the kill
list, will fight him _no matter what I do_.)

>      If you want to make named demons more interesting, here's
> my suggestion:  if one is defeated away from its home level, it
> is just banished back to that level instead of being removed
> from the game. 

This is one option, though it lends itself to the tactic of "bash Asmo
'til he hops the stairs, then descend and worry about him on the way
back up so I only fight him once, not twice."  But then, people do that
in vanilla anyway.

> And at most a tiny fraction of the usual amount
> of experience gets awarded, so that people won't be too tempted
> to treat repeatedly defeating them as an alternative to Rider
> farming.  Chasing them between levels will be more annoying :-}

Of course.  :)

> and summoning via same-race sacrifice won't be a shortcut for
> avoiding Juiblex (depending upon what if anything is done to
> handle the case where the home level gets created while the
> demon is already in play elsewhere).

This may be a more compelling reason to implement the no-permadeath
option; some of my chaotic characters have deliberately sacrificed
Minetown to make sure nobody could show up later that would annoy me.

As far as the case you mention, the demon should be summoned
back to the home level, though making sure the demon didn't get parked
somewhere in a saved level would be necessary (though you paragraphed
all that out below).  

>      Implementation could follow the Wizard's example and use
> the migrating monsters list.  The main drawback is that the
> ones who don't currently have a home level would need one,

Alternately, the ones who don't have a home level can be flagged as
such, and would revert to the Sanctum, say, if they were defeated once.

> requiring extra special levels and/or forcing the player to
> face all of them every game instead of seeing a surprise when
> one of the unguaranteed ones shows up.  Slash'em did that,
> and I don't think it's one of the better changes they've
> incorporated.  Knowing that you'll have to face Demogorgon
> is less interesting than having uncertainly about whether he
> might turn up.

Agreed.

(various other ideas snipped; random generation plus having those
generated monsters suddenly develop an affectation/longing for the
Sanctum might work out well in practice, and i agree that having
everyone have a guaranteed home level that always shows up is too 
slash'emy.)

Of course... if not all the levels were guaranteed to show up, it might
be viable to have something like _that_ in place; named demons whose
home exists revert to their home, and named demons who don't revert to
the Sanctum.  (Demo could be one of the few homeless demons.)

The prospect of fighting multiple demon lords upon descent into the
Sanctum could well give more weight to using whatever bribe is eventually
selected as 'suitable'.

